/* Pass 13 B: Maui civic and romance threads ch18-28. */
(function(){"use strict";const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const revisions={"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-18":{"number":18,"title":"One Door Closed by Choice","content":"The door to my old room was still there.\n\nI had avoided it for three days after the Lantern Walk, which was an impressive achievement considering it stood only a few turns from the square. It was an ordinary painted door set into a stretch of old stone. The Threadway did not advertise where it led. Only the small brass plate with my chosen name marked it as mine.\n\nMaui stopped beside me.\n\n\"Do you want me to come?\"\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI reached for the handle.\n\nThe brass was cold. When I turned it, a familiar smell met me: laundry soap, paper, and the faint dust of a room that had not been lived in for a while. The desk was where I remembered it. A book lay facedown beside a cup. The light came through the window at an angle I recognized without knowing why.\n\nThen I noticed the notebook.\n\nIt sat beneath the lamp, bound in faded green cloth, with a ribbon I could have tied myself. I knew the handwriting on the first page before I read the words.\n\nI had written a list of things I was afraid to ask for.\n\nThe first was simple: a day when I didn't have to explain myself.\n\nThe second had been crossed out so hard that the paper was nearly torn.\n\nThe third said: Don't let people decide where I belong just because they remember more than I do.\n\nI sat on the edge of the bed.\n\nI had expected the room to give me answers. Instead it gave me evidence of a person who had been frightened long before Lanternweald.\n\nFootsteps sounded in the hall behind me.\n\nMaui did not come through the door.\n\n\"Jasper?\" he called.\n\n\"I'm here.\"\n\n\"Are you all right?\"\n\nI looked at the notebook.\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\nThere was a long pause.\n\n\"Do you want me to wait?\"\n\n\"Yes. Outside.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nI heard him move away from the doorway. Not far, but far enough to give me space.\n\nThat was harder than rescue. I knew it was harder for him, because it was hard for me too.\n\nI stayed in the room for another twenty minutes. I opened the desk drawer and found a broken pen, two bus tickets, and a photograph turned facedown. I did not turn it over. I wasn't ready to look at a face I might not recognize.\n\nInstead I wrote the date in the notebook.\n\nI live somewhere else now.\n\nThe words looked absurdly small.\n\nI added: That does not mean this room has stopped being mine.\n\nWhen I finally stepped into the hall, Maui was sitting on the floor with his back against the wall. He had taken the opportunity to carve a tiny fish into the handle of his walking stick.\n\n\"You waited,\" I said.\n\n\"You asked.\"\n\n\"I thought you'd get bored.\"\n\n\"I did.\"\n\nHe held up the fish.\n\n\"I gave boredom a face.\"\n\nIt was a much better fish than the one on the broom handle.\n\nI laughed.\n\nThen I cried.\n\nThe tears surprised both of us. Maui stood, but he didn't reach for me until I stepped into his arms. His coat smelled of sea air and the smoke from Mara's bakery. I pressed my face against his shoulder and let the feelings come without arranging them into a story.\n\nAfter a while he asked, \"Do you want to go back in?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Do you want to close the door?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI turned, took the handle, and pulled it shut.\n\nThe latch clicked. Nothing in the arch dimmed. No bell sounded in protest. The Threadway did not punish me for leaving a room I wasn't ready to inhabit.\n\nI stood there for a moment, breathing.\n\n\"Is this running away?\" I asked.\n\nMaui considered the door.\n\n\"I don't think so.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you know where it is. And you're deciding when to return.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"That was a surprisingly good answer.\"\n\nHe grinned faintly.\n\n\"I'll try not to make a habit of it.\"\n\nWe walked back toward Tideglass in silence. Lanternwell Square smelled of bread and damp leaves. The frost was melting from the roofs. I noticed that the lantern I'd painted was still hanging beside the signal post, its wick extinguished and the circle of amber paint intact.\n\nBefore we reached home, I turned back toward the lantern by the signal post. The glass was misted from the cold. Somebody had replaced the wick while I was away, and the flame inside was steadier than the one I'd left.\n\n\"Who fixed it?\" I asked.\n\n\"Mara mentioned a volunteer on the morning shift,\" Maui said. \"They wrote it in the little service book.\"\n\nI found the book beneath the sheltered ledge. My earlier repair was still recorded there, along with the name of the person who had checked it today. The place didn't stop working when I stepped through a door.\n\n\"That's a strange thing to be relieved about,\" I said.\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"I suppose I thought everybody would be waiting for me to tell them what happened.\"\n\nMaui looked genuinely puzzled. \"We wanted you safe. That's not the same as wanting a report.\"\n\nThe words struck harder than I expected. I had a green notebook filled with memories of being useful, and somewhere in it a list I'd been afraid to show anyone. I didn't want those old obligations to walk out of the room with me and start issuing new orders.\n\nAt the house, I set my boots by the stove and looked at the fish carving Maui had left there. Its tail was uneven. He'd spent the afternoon sanding it rather than trying to follow me through the doorway.\n\n\"Can we have dinner before we talk?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And if I only tell you half of it?\"\n\n\"Then I'll know half.\"\n\n\"You're becoming dangerously sensible.\"\n\nHe grinned. \"Don't tell anyone.\"\n\nHe set out bread and warmed soup while I changed into dry clothes. Nothing about the food was magnificent, but I found myself hungry for the first time all day. When I returned, he had pulled out the chair I liked without making a ceremony of it.\n\nI took his hand briefly before we ate.\n\nI didn't owe him every word in the green notebook to be worthy of his company. I could choose what to share and still choose to stay.\n\nAt home, I put the green notebook on the kitchen table.\n\nMaui set down the fish carving beside it.\n\n\"Do you want to tell me what you found?\" he asked.\n\n\"Some of it.\"\n\n\"Which part?\"\n\n\"A list I wrote before I came here. Things I was afraid to ask for.\"\n\nHe sat opposite me.\n\n\"What did you ask for?\"\n\n\"Mostly nothing.\"\n\nHe winced.\n\n\"That's not very you.\"\n\n\"It was me. Maybe it still is, sometimes.\"\n\nHe thought about that.\n\n\"I've been trying to learn how to ask instead of deciding for people,\" he said. \"Maybe you're learning the same thing from the other side.\"\n\nI looked at the notebook.\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nI turned the page and wrote a new list. Not demands. Wishes.\n\nI want to visit the old room again when I choose.\n\nI want Maui to stay tonight.\n\nI want room for other people I love without making anyone a substitute.\n\nI want to know what the bell means, but not at the cost of my right to say no.\n\nI read the list aloud.\n\nMaui listened.\n\nWhen I finished, he said, \"I can say yes to one of those right now.\"\n\n\"Which?\"\n\n\"Staying.\"\n\nI felt heat rise in my face.\n\n\"You don't have to be so pleased with yourself.\"\n\n\"Impossible.\"\n\nHe reached for my hand across the table.\n\n\"Do you want company because you're sad,\" he asked, \"or because you want me?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Both. Is that allowed?\"\n\nHe smiled, but there was no triumph in it.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nI went around the table and sat beside him. He kissed me softly, then paused when I caught his collar and drew him closer. The room was warm from the little fire. Outside, the sea moved in steady breaths along the stones.\n\nWe talked for another hour, about the old notebook and the strange relief of leaving something unfinished. We laughed about his carved fish, which had a suspicious resemblance to Fen.\n\nWhen I told him I wanted to go upstairs, he asked once more whether I was sure.\n\n\"I am.\"\n\nWe left the notebook on the table and closed the bedroom door. The rest of the night belonged to us, with no demand to make grief beautiful.\n\nIn the morning, the notebook was still there beside the little wooden fish.\n\nI opened to the page I'd written.\n\nUnder the list, Maui had added a sentence in enormous handwriting.\n\nYOU CAN ASK ME AGAIN TOMORROW.\n\nI laughed so hard I nearly spilled my tea.\n\nThen I wrote beneath it, in much smaller letters:\n\nI probably will.","word_count":1494},"maui-22":{"number":22,"title":"Tea, Salt and Smoke","content":"Iroh arrived at Tideglass Cove carrying three teacups, a brass timer, and enough patience to make me suspicious.\n\nMaui was beside the new sea-glass orchard trying to light a cooking fire with wood he had collected from the beach. The first piece was damp. The second was salt-soaked. The third produced a smell like someone had set a wet boot on fire.\n\n\"Why won't it catch?\" he demanded.\n\n\"Because you're trying to burn the sea,\" I said.\n\n\"Very poetic. Not useful.\"\n\nIroh watched from the path.\n\n\"Would you like some dry kindling?\" he asked.\n\nMaui straightened.\n\n\"I can make fire.\"\n\n\"I have no doubt. The question is whether you wish to make smoke first.\"\n\nMaui regarded the dark plume rising from his little heap of wood.\n\n\"Perhaps I do not.\"\n\nIroh set down the cups. He had come to discuss planting herbs along the sheltered wall, but Fen had asked him to wait until the runoff from the new beds was tested. The salt level had dropped, though not enough for every cutting he wanted to grow.\n\nWe had an hour before the measurement.\n\nIroh opened a tin.\n\n\"Tea?\"\n\nMaui eyed it.\n\n\"Is it strong?\"\n\n\"Strong enough for conversation.\"\n\n\"Then we'll need a bigger pot.\"\n\nI sat beside the cold fire. Maui reluctantly followed. Iroh handed him the brass timer, a small device with a dial and a faint ticking sound.\n\n\"What is this?\"\n\n\"A reminder that leaves require time in water.\"\n\nMaui turned the timer over.\n\n\"I could make the water hotter.\"\n\n\"Then you might burn the leaves.\"\n\n\"I could make the leaves tougher.\"\n\n\"I recommend asking them first.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nMaui looked between us.\n\n\"Are you two doing this on purpose?\"\n\n\"Very little in life is entirely unplanned,\" Iroh said.\n\nHe poured the water and set the timer.\n\nWhile we waited, Fen came down the path with the garden's salinity report. The sheltered bed was suitable for rosemary and sea kale, but the lower edge remained too brackish for the mint Iroh had hoped to plant.\n\n\"We'll keep the mint at Jasmine Steps,\" he said easily.\n\nMaui looked impressed.\n\n\"You're not angry?\"\n\n\"Why should I be?\"\n\n\"You carried it all the way here.\"\n\n\"The plant will be happier elsewhere.\"\n\nI looked at Maui.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Don't say it,\" he warned.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"You were thinking something about the orchard.\"\n\n\"I contain many thoughts.\"\n\nIroh's eyes crinkled.\n\n\"Then you have plenty of material for an evening.\"\n\nThe timer rang.\n\nMaui nearly dropped it.\n\n\"That's a very small bell.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" Iroh said.\n\nThe word changed the air.\n\nWe had all heard the second bell beneath the cove several days earlier. It was still being measured. Fen had closed the lowest route while Danse and Tamsin examined the old structure. Neri had admitted the passage was sealed during the Closed Bells, though they still resisted naming everyone responsible.\n\nMaui looked toward the water.\n\n\"Could the old bell be like this?\"\n\n\"Perhaps,\" Iroh said. \"But a bell is only a bell until we know who placed it and why.\"\n\n\"That sounds less exciting than a sea spirit.\"\n\n\"It is also easier to inspect.\"\n\nI picked up my cup.\n\nThe tea tasted of toasted grain and citrus, the affordable house blend Iroh had developed after the poor mountain harvest. It wasn't the rare mint, and it didn't need to pretend it was.\n\n\"Good,\" I said.\n\nIroh smiled.\n\nMaui sipped, considered, and said, \"I could drink six cups.\"\n\n\"You may have one more.\"\n\n\"Why only one?\"\n\n\"Because you are already remarkably energetic.\"\n\nMaui laughed.\n\nFen came back from the drainage channel with mud on their sleeve and a request for two more buckets of clean water. The rosemary cuttings had been placed in the right bed, but the path to it was still slick.\n\nMaui started to leap toward the shed. Iroh lifted one hand.\n\n\"Perhaps we should ask where the buckets are wanted before we carry them.\"\n\nFen looked from one to the other. \"By the north path, not beside the stove. And don't pour them on the seedlings until I check the soil.\"\n\nMaui stopped. \"A man could lose his whole heroic reputation carrying water incorrectly.\"\n\n\"An alarming risk,\" Iroh said.\n\nI laughed and went to fetch the buckets with him. The two men watched one another with a sort of guarded curiosity. Maui clearly wanted to impress Iroh, who had survived battles and chosen to run a tea shop. Iroh seemed amused by Maui's inability to let a quiet moment remain quiet.\n\nLater, while the fire pit cooled, Iroh asked if I intended to stay at Tideglass that evening.\n\n\"I do,\" I said. \"I have time for tea with you tomorrow if you'd like, but I don't want either of you assuming I can be in two places at once.\"\n\nMaui picked up his cup. \"That seems a shame. I'd be magnificent at sharing a schedule with two Jaspers.\"\n\n\"One of me is more than enough.\"\n\nIroh chuckled. \"You have been very clear about preferring polyamory and ethical non-monogamy. An invitation is not diminished because it has to be accepted.\"\n\nMaui looked down at his own hands. \"I know that. Sometimes I'm still figuring out how to say I wish I had more time.\"\n\n\"Then say exactly that,\" I told him.\n\nHe met my eyes. \"I wish I had more time.\"\n\nThe simple answer made my throat tighten. I squeezed his hand beneath the table, and Iroh politely became interested in rescuing a cup from the breeze.\n\nWhen we rose to help clean the cooking area, neither man needed to prove himself better at warmth, wisdom or carrying stones. The tea had gone cold. The company hadn't.\n\nThe afternoon stretched out with ordinary work. We moved the dry rosemary cutting to the proper bed. I helped Fen check the drainage channel. Maui carried a crate of stones and, to his credit, did not turn the job into a challenge.\n\nBy evening the wind had shifted toward the shore. Iroh stayed to help us light a small cooking fire using properly dried wood and an ash pit set away from the growing beds. He explained the difference between a fire that warms people and one built to impress them.\n\n\"Do you ever want to impress someone?\" Maui asked.\n\nIroh looked at me.\n\n\"Frequently. Usually I make a terrible fool of myself.\"\n\nI felt my cheeks warm.\n\nMaui grinned.\n\n\"So do I.\"\n\nI stared at both of them.\n\n\"I have no idea why I spend time with either of you.\"\n\n\"Excellent judgment,\" Iroh said.\n\n\"Terrible taste,\" Maui added.\n\nThey laughed like men who had agreed not to compete for the evening. That mattered more to me than a perfect speech about how modern our relationships were.\n\nAfter supper, Iroh returned to Jasmine Steps. He had work in the morning and wanted time to prepare it. We walked him to the upper path.\n\n\"Thank you for the tea,\" I said.\n\n\"Thank you for the company.\"\n\nHe touched my hand, warm and gentle, and waited until I returned the gesture. Maui stood a respectful distance away, checking the lanterns along the path.\n\n\"I'll see you at the pantry tomorrow,\" I said.\n\n\"I look forward to it.\"\n\nIroh bowed his head to Maui.\n\n\"Be gentle with your fire.\"\n\n\"Only if it behaves.\"\n\nThey smiled at each other.\n\nWhen Iroh had gone, Maui fell into step beside me.\n\n\"He's good at waiting,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I'm trying.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe was quiet for a while. The smoke from the cooking fire lingered faintly in our clothes. We passed the garden, where the sea kale stood in neat rows and the dry-slope herbs tilted toward the last light.\n\n\"Can I say something foolish?\" he asked.\n\n\"Always.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I wonder how you can love more than one person and still know which way to walk home.\"\n\nI stopped.\n\n\"Do you mean tonight?\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"I don't love Iroh because you're missing something. I don't love you because anyone else is missing something. I care about different people for different reasons, and I've chosen to be honest about that. Tonight I want to come home with you.\"\n\nHe studied my face.\n\n\"And tomorrow?\"\n\n\"Tomorrow I have tea with Iroh. And then I'll decide how to spend the rest of the day. The agreement isn't that everybody gets the same hours. It's that we tell each other what we can actually offer.\"\n\nMaui breathed out.\n\n\"That makes sense.\"\n\n\"Does it feel okay?\"\n\n\"Mostly.\"\n\n\"Mostly is a good beginning.\"\n\nHe smiled, a little shy.\n\n\"I want to be the person you can tell when mostly isn't enough.\"\n\n\"You already are.\"\n\nI stepped closer. He leaned down to meet me, and the kiss was warm and slow, tasting faintly of citrus tea. For once neither of us had a clever remark ready.\n\nWe walked home after the lanterns were lit.\n\nThe little brass timer stayed with Iroh. The second bell remained a mystery beneath the tide.\n\nI was learning that patience could be an act, not just the absence of movement.","word_count":1516},"maui-24":{"number":24,"title":"A Place to Put Jealousy","content":"The letter was folded into the shape of a bird.\n\nIt had been left on the Lanternwell notice board with my name written in careful blue ink. Inside was an invitation to spend an afternoon at Moonroot with Iroh, reviewing tea-stained maps of the old terraces. He had drawn a tiny teacup at the bottom and, beneath it, written that the invitation was entirely optional.\n\nMaui read over my shoulder.\n\n\"A map date,\" he said.\n\n\"An archive visit.\"\n\n\"With tea.\"\n\n\"Those things can happen together.\"\n\nHe smiled. I waited for the joke. None came.\n\nWe were in Tideglass Cove a little later when he dropped the same length of rope twice. He wasn't usually clumsy with rope. That was why I noticed.\n\n\"Is your hand hurting again?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then what's wrong?\"\n\nHe pulled the line through the ring and tightened it much harder than he needed to.\n\n\"Nothing.\"\n\nI put down the tide chart.\n\n\"Maui.\"\n\nHis shoulders lowered.\n\n\"Can we talk somewhere people aren't measuring knots?\"\n\nWe walked to the little shelter above the sea-glass orchard. One raised bed was sprouting; the second was still bare because Fen had refused to transplant anything until the salt reading changed. The bench Greg and Maui had built sat beside the path, facing the water. It had no plaque, though Maui had carved a tiny fish beneath one armrest.\n\nHe stood instead of sitting.\n\n\"I know about Iroh,\" he said.\n\n\"I know you do.\"\n\n\"I know about the others. We talked about being ethically non-monogamous. You told me that you prefer polyamory, and I said I wanted to try being part of your life without treating it as a race.\"\n\n\"You did.\"\n\n\"I meant it.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"But sometimes,\" he admitted, \"I hear that you've been invited somewhere, and part of me starts inventing a story where everybody else gets the best parts of you.\"\n\nThe wind lifted a strand of hair across my face.\n\n\"Which parts?\"\n\n\"The ones that aren't tired from fixing the village. The ones that laugh because someone said something clever and not because you're trying to make me feel better.\"\n\nHis voice was almost embarrassed.\n\nI wanted to tell him he was wrong. I wanted to assure him that I laughed more with him, or loved him more, or somehow held a private scale on which I could prove he wasn't losing.\n\nAny of those answers would have made the feeling smaller at the cost of making somebody else's love a competition.\n\n\"I laugh with you because you're funny,\" I said. \"Sometimes because you're ridiculous. Those are not consolation laughs.\"\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n\"Mostly ridiculous.\"\n\n\"Frequently.\"\n\nHe sat down at last.\n\n\"What do I do with this feeling? I don't want to tell you who you may see.\"\n\n\"You can tell me when you're afraid. You can ask for time together. You can ask whether a plan has changed. But I can't promise never to love anyone else, and I won't ask them to disappear to make us feel safe.\"\n\nMaui stared at the tide.\n\n\"I don't want that either.\"\n\n\"I believe you.\"\n\nHe looked up.\n\n\"You do?\"\n\n\"Yes. Wanting reassurance isn't the same thing as wanting control. It's what you do with it that matters.\"\n\nHe leaned back.\n\n\"That's inconvenient. I was hoping there'd be a monster to fight.\"\n\n\"Jealousy doesn't have claws, Maui.\"\n\n\"Mine might.\"\n\nI laughed, and this time he laughed with me.\n\nWe sat until the breeze grew cold. I told him I could make time for a meal after my archive visit, but only if I finished when I'd said I would and didn't overpromise to Iroh. He told me he wanted us to have a day with no errands, no celebrations, and no people asking him to carry things.\n\n\"A day of doing nothing?\" I asked.\n\n\"Not nothing.\"\n\n\"What, then?\"\n\n\"Whatever we choose after breakfast.\"\n\nThat sounded wonderful.\n\nWe put it on the calendar.\n\nAt Moonroot, the map Iroh had invited me to see was spread beneath a weighted sheet of glass. A corner had been damaged by an old leak, leaving the ink blurred where the terrace boundaries met.\n\n\"This is what I wanted your opinion on,\" he said. \"It appears someone redrew a garden path after the flood. I don't know whether the new route was ever used.\"\n\nI studied the line. The nearest record showed an invoice for gravel, but no confirmation that the work had been done. Iroh didn't ask me to solve it with a flash of remembered knowledge. He asked which evidence we should look for next.\n\nI thought of Maui's rope, dropped twice, and of how jealous he'd sounded without wanting to make me responsible for fixing the feeling.\n\n\"Can I tell you something without making it your problem?\" I asked.\n\n\"Certainly.\"\n\n\"Maui was afraid I would enjoy this afternoon so much that I'd stop wanting days with him.\"\n\nIroh set his cup down. \"Do you want me to reassure him?\"\n\n\"No. That's between us. I wanted you to know why I might seem distracted.\"\n\nHe nodded. \"Then we can be distracted together, briefly, and return to the map.\"\n\nWe sat in silence until I remembered the gravel invoice. Iroh smiled at the practical suggestion. It was one of the ways he differed from Maui: where Maui wanted to move the mountain, Iroh sometimes wanted to examine the date written at its foot.\n\n\"I like both of you,\" I said. \"In different ways. I've been explicit that I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. I don't want either affection turned into a test the other has to pass.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps we should both resist the temptation to be the most reassuring person in the room.\"\n\nI laughed. \"You make that sound easy.\"\n\n\"Oh, it isn't. I am extraordinarily good at giving advice nobody requested.\"\n\nHe touched my hand, then waited. I leaned closer, enough to tell him the invitation was welcome. We kissed briefly beside the map table before returning to the damaged record. Neither of us claimed to have fixed jealousy or the old terrace in one afternoon.\n\nOn my way to Moonroot, I stopped at the notice board. The old departure ledger had been updated with a sign explaining how travelers could request private help leaving without surrendering their destination. Somebody had placed a bowl of chalk beside it so visitors could mark whether they'd already received assistance, then erase the mark themselves.\n\nI thought about how much had changed since everyone treated the arrival book like a list of people the vale owned.\n\nAt Moonroot, Iroh greeted me with tea and a stack of maps.\n\n\"You look thoughtful,\" he said.\n\n\"Maui and I had a difficult conversation.\"\n\n\"Would you like to tell me?\"\n\n\"Some of it.\"\n\nHe listened without asking me to make a comparison between them. We examined the old terrace drains, discovered a marking that might indicate a seasonal overflow, and argued gently over whether a particular line was a road or a watercourse.\n\nWhen I returned to Tideglass, Maui was cooking. Properly dried kindling burned beneath a pot of vegetables, and he'd set two places at the table.\n\n\"How was your date?\" he asked.\n\n\"Good. We found a possible drainage mark.\"\n\n\"Romantic.\"\n\n\"In its way.\"\n\nHe studied me.\n\n\"Did you have a good time?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHis expression softened.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nI went around the table and kissed his cheek.\n\nHe turned toward me, surprised.\n\n\"Was that a reassurance kiss?\"\n\n\"No,\" I said. \"That was a kiss because I wanted to kiss you.\"\n\n\"Important distinction.\"\n\n\"Extremely.\"\n\nHe asked if I wanted another.\n\nI did.\n\nWe burned the first piece of bread because neither of us remembered the stove. Later, with dinner rescued and the windows open to the sea, he told me he'd still felt jealous for a few minutes after I left.\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And then I went to see Fen about the ropes. I didn't make you come home early. I didn't ask Iroh to change his plans.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"That sounds like a good place to put the feeling.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Somewhere it can be heard without becoming the person in charge.\"\n\nHe considered that, then pulled me close enough that I could rest my forehead against his.\n\n\"Can I ask for something else?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"That day without errands.\"\n\n\"It's on the calendar.\"\n\n\"Can it start with sleeping late?\"\n\n\"Absolutely.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\nThat night we closed the kitchen door, left the dishes soaking, and kept the remainder of our time private. The next morning the dishes were still there. So was the calendar.\n\nWe washed them together, and neither of us pretended we'd cured jealousy forever.","word_count":1437},"maui-25":{"number":25,"title":"Mereana Returns","content":"Mereana's boat arrived without a banner.\n\nThat was the first thing I noticed. The second was that she stepped onto the dock carrying a toolbox, not an instrument.\n\nMaui had been expecting a performance. He'd told three different people that the visiting artists from Aotearoa might be bringing a celebration to the cove, and by noon half the village was hovering near the landing.\n\nMereana looked at the crowd and raised one eyebrow.\n\n\"Did someone announce a festival?\"\n\nMaui looked at me.\n\nI looked at Maui.\n\n\"Apparently,\" I said, \"someone did.\"\n\nHer group had come to inspect a set of carved panels they had lent to the local heritage workshop. They were returning after an exhibition elsewhere, and Mereana had scheduled a short visit because she wanted to see how the panels were being stored and whether the agreement still suited her group.\n\nIt was their work. They could choose when to display it, discuss it, or take it home.\n\nPip hurried down the steps carrying a clipboard.\n\n\"We prepared the hall,\" they said. \"We can set up chairs if you'd like to perform.\"\n\n\"That's kind,\" Mereana answered. \"But we didn't come to perform.\"\n\nThe disappointment moved through the crowd like a breeze.\n\nA visitor standing near the rope store spoke before I could.\n\n\"But aren't you here to do the traditional courtship dance? Someone told me your group teaches the old way people choose partners.\"\n\nMereana went still.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nThe visitor blinked.\n\n\"I read somewhere—\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"I don't remember.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps don't explain my people to me from a thing you can't remember.\"\n\nHer voice wasn't raised. She didn't need it to be.\n\nI felt my face grow hot on her behalf, which was a familiar and unhelpful sensation. I wanted to step in, make a speech about respect, and rescue everyone from the silence.\n\nMereana was perfectly capable of responding herself.\n\nI stayed beside the supply cart.\n\nThe visitor looked uncomfortable.\n\n\"I didn't mean offense.\"\n\n\"Then you can ask about what we're actually doing,\" Mereana said. \"We are artists. Some of our work draws on specific traditions with specific histories. Those aren't interchangeable with a universal romance ritual, and they aren't yours to assign us because you've come expecting entertainment.\"\n\nPip lowered the clipboard.\n\n\"Would you prefer we close the hall to the public?\"\n\n\"Not necessarily. I'd like to inspect the panels. Then we'll decide whether there's anything we want to share.\"\n\nMaui stepped forward.\n\n\"I told people you might perform. I shouldn't have.\"\n\nMereana looked at him.\n\n\"No, you shouldn't.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"I'll correct it.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThat could have been the end of the matter, except the panels had been moved.\n\nNot stolen. Not damaged deliberately. Someone had relocated them to a storage room where the air was drier, without telling Mereana's group. The new room was locked, and the person with the key was away at the market.\n\nMereana looked at the empty display rack.\n\n\"That wasn't our agreement.\"\n\nNeri arrived with the lending record and apologized. There was no excuse to invent. The workshop had made a decision about someone else's work without consulting its owners.\n\nWe found the keyholder. We inspected the storage room. Two panels were fine, one had warped slightly against a support that put pressure on its edge, and the fourth had been wrapped in fabric that trapped moisture.\n\nMereana took notes.\n\n\"Will it recover?\" I asked.\n\n\"Possibly. We need to examine it properly.\"\n\nThe workshop steward offered to pay for a repair.\n\nMereana nodded.\n\n\"That's part of it. The other part is changing the handling process.\"\n\nShe asked for a revised lending form that named who could move the work, under what conditions, and how the owners would be contacted. Neri agreed to help draft it. Tamsin volunteered to make a better storage rack, but only after Mereana approved the design.\n\nMaui carried the old rack outside.\n\nHe didn't make a speech.\n\nI found him by the door, watching the group examine the panels.\n\n\"Still wish it were a festival?\" I asked.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n\"I like hearing people share beautiful things. I just keep forgetting that choosing not to share is part of the beauty too.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\nMereana accepted the tea, then asked whether the chairs could be moved to somewhere the wind wouldn't blow dust into the cups. We carried them into the workshop's small courtyard, where a canvas awning had been pulled taut between two posts.\n\n\"I was worried you'd leave angry,\" Maui admitted.\n\n\"I was angry,\" she answered. \"I also wanted the panels checked. Both things can be true.\"\n\nHe looked at her over the rim of his cup.\n\n\"The work means something to the people who made it,\" she continued. \"Some of them are here, some couldn't travel. I was entrusted to speak about the loan. That doesn't make me the owner of everyone's story.\"\n\nNeri returned with a draft form. Mereana read the first page and pointed out that it included permission to photograph the panels for publicity. Her group had agreed to conservation photographs, not promotional images.\n\nNeri crossed out the sentence and wrote two separate options.\n\nMaui watched. \"I thought the village would be honored to display them.\"\n\n\"It was,\" Mereana said. \"But honor isn't the same as permission.\"\n\nWhen she excused herself to speak to the other artists, he slumped in his chair.\n\n\"I wanted them to feel celebrated.\"\n\n\"I know. Next time ask what celebration they want.\"\n\nHe laughed without much humor. \"Your next sentence is going to be that I don't get to choose the music.\"\n\n\"You can choose what music you play yourself.\"\n\nHe considered that, then drew a small circle on the table with his fingertip.\n\n\"Will you still dance with me after I embarrass myself?\"\n\n\"If you ask me and I want to.\"\n\nHe looked up, relief plain on his face. \"I will ask.\"\n\nWe helped carry the repaired rack back inside after Mereana approved its position. She checked the supports herself. The next morning's signed form would matter more than anything Maui could have announced from a stage, but the repaired storage space was a kind of welcome too.\n\nA little later Mereana joined us outside. She seemed tired, but not angry enough to leave immediately.\n\n\"Would you like tea?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes. And a chair.\"\n\nI brought both.\n\nShe sat beneath the awning and watched the harbor.\n\n\"I don't mind people being curious,\" she said. \"I mind when curiosity arrives with a costume already picked out for me.\"\n\nMaui looked at his hands.\n\n\"I've done that to people.\"\n\n\"We all can,\" she said. \"The question is whether we notice, and what we do afterward.\"\n\nI thought of my own missing memories, the way strangers sometimes decided who I must have been from a few fragments. I understood the shape of her frustration without pretending the histories were the same.\n\nMereana turned toward me.\n\n\"How is the new garden?\"\n\n\"Small. Salty. Stubborn.\"\n\n\"Sounds like a place worth seeing.\"\n\nWe walked there together after the storage inspection. Fen explained the beds and how visitors could contribute without importing plants that damaged the dunes. Mereana was delighted by the little flowers growing untouched beside the signal post.\n\n\"Those were here before the beds?\" she asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" Fen said.\n\n\"Good. I like a place that leaves room for what was already living.\"\n\nBy sunset, the visiting group had decided to stay one more day to work on the storage agreement. They did not perform. Nobody pressured them.\n\nMaui cooked supper. Mereana told us about a boat repair she had supervised back home, mostly to complain about how much easier it was to give advice than to keep a project on budget. Maui confessed he was learning the same thing.\n\nShe laughed.\n\nWhen the meal ended, she stood and stretched.\n\n\"Thank you for asking what we wanted,\" she said.\n\nI looked at the untouched instruments beside her bag.\n\n\"Thank you for trusting us enough to tell us no.\"\n\nAfter she left, Maui and I washed the cups.\n\n\"Was that a successful visit?\" he asked.\n\n\"She got her work inspected, the process changed, and she chose to stay. I'd say yes.\"\n\n\"No performance.\"\n\n\"No performance.\"\n\nHe turned toward me.\n\n\"Can I sing?\"\n\nI stared at him.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI kissed him before he could begin anyway.\n\nThe following morning, the lending form had new boxes for consent to move, display, repair, and return the panels. None were checked by default.\n\nMereana read it, made three corrections, and signed only the parts she'd agreed to.\n\nThat was the kind of welcome worth keeping.","word_count":1440},"maui-26":{"number":26,"title":"The Price of Welcome","content":"The pantry shelves looked fuller from the doorway.\n\nIt was an optical illusion caused by the jars in the front row. Behind them, the flour sack was nearly empty, the last dried beans had been marked for the clinic, and the vegetable crates contained more leaves than vegetables.\n\nMara stood with the inventory sheet in her hand.\n\n\"Don't say it's fine,\" she warned.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"Everyone does.\"\n\nI stepped beside her and looked at the figures.\n\nA flood upstream had blocked the lower cart route for several days. Deliveries were using the longer ridge path. The pantry had fed the repair crews, provided emergency meals for two visiting families, and supplied the clinic volunteers without charging people who had arrived with nothing.\n\nNobody had done anything wrong by needing food.\n\nBut the jars didn't refill because we had been kind.\n\nMaui appeared carrying his hook.\n\n\"I'll fish.\"\n\nMara looked at him.\n\n\"How many?\"\n\n\"As many as we need.\"\n\n\"Who cleans them? Who salts them? Who stores them? Who makes sure the people who can't eat fish have something else?\"\n\nMaui blinked.\n\n\"Fish is food.\"\n\n\"Not the only food.\"\n\nI heard Katara's voice behind us.\n\n\"And a fresh catch won't survive a week in warm weather without proper handling.\"\n\nShe had come to check the clinic supplies and brought two volunteers with her. One of them had written down the meals people could actually eat, including allergies and religious or personal restrictions they had chosen to disclose.\n\nKatara put the sheet on the table.\n\n\"Let's start with what the families need. Then we can decide which supplies will help.\"\n\nMaui looked disappointed.\n\n\"Fishing still helps.\"\n\n\"Of course it does,\" Katara said. \"If the work after catching the fish is planned.\"\n\nThat was fair. It was also not a thrilling beginning to an emergency.\n\nWe called a pantry meeting.\n\nMara brought the ledger. Fen brought the tide chart and information about the safest fishing hours. Tamsin described which carts could use the ridge. Iroh offered an inexpensive soup recipe that used less fuel than several smaller meals. Greg volunteered to help distribute food but asked to be given a definite shift rather than a promise to appear whenever needed.\n\nEdda spoke about delivery access. Some families could reach the square, others couldn't. A meal on a high shelf wasn't useful to a person who couldn't lift it down.\n\nI made a list of offers.\n\nThen I caught myself assigning names to jobs.\n\n\"Wait,\" I said.\n\nEveryone looked up.\n\n\"I've written Greg on the evening delivery without asking whether he's free.\"\n\nGreg raised a hand.\n\n\"I'm not. I said tomorrow morning.\"\n\nI crossed out the shift.\n\n\"Sorry.\"\n\nMara grinned.\n\n\"Congratulations. You caught yourself before we had to.\"\n\nThe meeting took an hour. We planned three days of meals, set aside the clinic's medically necessary supplies, and wrote a public notice explaining which items were short without listing which families had requested help.\n\nMaui agreed to fish with Fen during the safe tide window. Two people who knew food handling volunteered to prepare the catch, and Mara allocated salt from the bakery stores with a clear repayment plan. The pantry would buy vegetables from the next available cart rather than assume someone would donate them.\n\nNone of the numbers were comfortable.\n\nThey were workable.\n\nThe next morning I went with Maui to the landing.\n\nHe was quiet while Fen checked the lines. The sea was calm, but the water carried branches and silt from the upstream flood. We used the upper route and kept the boat close to shore.\n\nMaui caught three fish.\n\nThen he stopped.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Only three?\"\n\n\"Fen says the small ones need to stay.\"\n\nFen nodded.\n\n\"Breeding season. Take the adults, leave the young, and we might still have fish next month.\"\n\nMaui looked at the water.\n\n\"I used to think feeding people meant bringing home the biggest possible catch.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now it means making sure the sea can feed them again.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nWe took the fish to the cleaning station, where Mara's cousin was waiting. Maui offered to help with the work. She handed him an apron.\n\nHe stared at it.\n\n\"You want me to wear that?\"\n\n\"Unless you enjoy smelling like fish for three days.\"\n\nHe put it on.\n\nBy afternoon the first meals were ready. The clinic received its share. The two visiting families chose from the available options, without having to explain publicly why they needed help. Edda checked that the delivery station was accessible from the level path.\n\nIroh asked Mara whether she wanted more soup tomorrow. She studied the pantry list before answering.\n\n\"Not unless you can bring clean jars back,\" she said. \"We don't have enough containers for another large delivery.\"\n\n\"Then I shall bring the soup in what I have and collect the empties after.\"\n\nMara nodded. I watched how the exchange left both people free to decide what they could actually provide. There was no miracle pot that filled itself, and Iroh didn't pretend his tea shop could feed a valley forever.\n\nA child arrived with a note from the clinic, asking whether the next delivery could include something easier to carry up the hill. Edda checked the route before we agreed to a new time. Maui looked at the note and then toward the sea.\n\n\"I could deliver it in one trip,\" he said.\n\n\"Probably,\" I answered. \"But can the next volunteer do the same?\"\n\nHe made a face. \"You're ruining all the fun.\"\n\n\"Just the kind that requires you to be here every time.\"\n\nWe divided the delivery into smaller loads that ordinary people could carry. Mara recorded the change; a new worker would be able to follow it without having to guess what we'd intended.\n\nWhen we finished, the apron around Maui's waist was damp and smelled terrible. I couldn't stop smiling at him.\n\n\"What?\" he demanded.\n\n\"Nothing. You're a fearsome fisher.\"\n\n\"I am a legend.\"\n\n\"With fish scales in your hair.\"\n\nHe tried to brush them away and made it worse. I laughed so hard I had to hold the edge of the table.\n\nHis eyes softened. \"I like making you laugh.\"\n\n\"I like laughing.\"\n\n\"Can I kiss you before the work starts again?\"\n\nI nodded, and he leaned close enough that I could smell salt and clean soap beneath the fish. The kiss was brief, gentle, and decidedly less heroic than anything he'd planned for the morning.\n\nThen Mara called us back to the loading list. The pantry remained short on flour, but its deliveries had a plan that didn't require our presence at every meal. It felt like a better kind of generosity.\n\nIroh brought a pot of soup.\n\nToph tasted it and declared it \"suspiciously sensible.\"\n\nKatara laughed.\n\nThe pantry wasn't saved forever. A storm could block the ridge again. A harvest could fail. Someone could need more help than we'd planned.\n\nThat was why we wrote down what worked and what didn't.\n\nAfter dinner, Mara counted the remaining stock.\n\n\"We have enough for the next three days,\" she said.\n\n\"Just three?\"\n\n\"Three is real. A week would be a promise we haven't earned.\"\n\nI looked at the figures.\n\nFor once, I didn't try to make them prettier.\n\nMaui found me at the table after the others left.\n\n\"You're worried.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"About the pantry?\"\n\n\"About how often welcome means somebody quietly doing more than they can afford.\"\n\nHe sat beside me.\n\n\"I thought I could solve it by fishing all night.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I like doing something big.\"\n\n\"You also wore an apron today.\"\n\nHe groaned.\n\n\"Do not tell anyone.\"\n\n\"I have already told three people.\"\n\n\"Jasper.\"\n\nI laughed and leaned against him.\n\nHe turned his face toward mine.\n\n\"Would you like me to stay with you tonight?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Quietly?\"\n\n\"Mostly.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nHe kissed me softly, then kissed me again when I caught his sleeve. There was flour on my hands and a smear of salt on his wrist. I had never found a pantry inventory particularly romantic before.\n\nBut there was something tender about a person willing to do the ordinary part of caring.\n\nThe next morning we went back to the square to help unload the ridge cart. It was late, muddy, and carrying fewer vegetables than we'd hoped.\n\nMara looked at the crates and sighed.\n\nThen she opened the ledger.\n\nWe began again.","word_count":1401},"maui-27":{"number":27,"title":"The Visitor's Right to Leave","content":"The boy was sitting on his suitcase when I found him.\n\nHe couldn't have been more than nineteen, though his boots were worn in the way boots get when someone has been walking without much choice about where. His name was Tomas. He'd arrived through the northern Threadway three days ago and spent most of that time helping at the pantry. Now he was waiting on the steps beneath the arch.\n\nBeside him stood a woman named Vel, who had started calling Maui the Guardian of Tideglass after seeing him catch a boat during the mooring accident.\n\n\"You don't understand,\" Vel was telling him. \"People made room for you here. You can't just leave after they've helped.\"\n\nTomas stared at his shoes.\n\n\"I need to go home.\"\n\n\"Then why come at all?\"\n\nI stopped close enough to be heard without coming between them.\n\n\"Because people are allowed to visit,\" I said.\n\nVel turned toward me.\n\n\"The community fed him.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And gave him a bed.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And now he just walks out?\"\n\nI looked at Tomas.\n\n\"Do you want help reaching your door?\"\n\nHe nodded without looking up.\n\n\"All right. I'll check the safe route.\"\n\nVel put a hand on the suitcase handle.\n\n\"Someone should tell Maui.\"\n\nI felt my shoulders tighten.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"He's the one who welcomed the newcomers. He'll understand.\"\n\nTomas pulled his suitcase back.\n\n\"Please don't make me explain to him.\"\n\nIt was barely louder than the sea.\n\nI took a breath. Then I asked Vel to release the handle. She did, reluctantly.\n\n\"Neri has a private departure service,\" I said. \"No one has to explain their reasons to a crowd.\"\n\nVel looked hurt.\n\n\"I thought this was a place where people cared.\"\n\n\"It is. Caring doesn't make the doors ours.\"\n\nI escorted Tomas to the notice board. The departure instructions were clear, but somebody had hung a festival banner across half the text. I moved it aside. Neri was on duty and listened as Tomas asked for a route without giving an address.\n\n\"Would you like a guide to the arch?\" they asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Would you like us to keep your destination private?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then that's what we'll do.\"\n\nMaui arrived before we left.\n\nNot because I'd summoned him. Vel had.\n\nHe came down the square with his hook over one shoulder and a confused expression.\n\n\"What's happening?\"\n\nVel stepped toward him.\n\n\"Tell him he should stay. We welcomed him.\"\n\nMaui looked at the suitcase, then at Tomas, then at me.\n\nI didn't rescue him from the choice.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said, \"do you want to go home?\"\n\nTomas nodded.\n\n\"Then do you need anything for the journey?\"\n\nVel stared.\n\n\"But the pantry—\"\n\n\"The pantry helped because he needed help,\" Maui said. \"Not because we bought another member of the village.\"\n\nHe said it plainly. No thunder. No boast.\n\nVel looked at him like he'd broken something she loved.\n\n\"I thought you understood loyalty.\"\n\n\"I do.\"\n\n\"Then why aren't you asking him to stay?\"\n\n\"Because I wouldn't want someone to keep me somewhere by calling it gratitude.\"\n\nTomas looked up for the first time.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he whispered.\n\nWe walked him to the arch along the safe route. Neri came too, carrying the travel map. The Threadway glowed faintly in the stone, not bright enough for a spectacle. Tomas put his suitcase down, checked that he had his papers, and asked whether he could come back someday.\n\n\"If you want,\" Neri said.\n\nHe looked at Maui.\n\nMaui smiled.\n\n\"We'll still have terrible rope and decent bread.\"\n\nTomas laughed.\n\nThen he stepped through.\n\nThe light settled. The arch remained open for other travelers.\n\nVel had followed us at a distance. When Tomas disappeared, she turned away without speaking.\n\nAfter Tomas left, Neri took the public arrival register back to the office. They didn't cross out his name as though he'd never been there. They marked the departure as confirmed, with no destination printed for curious strangers.\n\nA small girl came up to the notice and asked whether Tomas had gone away because he didn't like the village.\n\n\"Maybe he missed home,\" I said. \"He was allowed to go even if he liked us very much.\"\n\nShe considered that. \"My grandmother goes home after tea.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Nobody gets mad at her.\"\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nThe girl ran off toward the bakery. I wished I could explain the matter as simply to the adults.\n\nMaui watched Neri lock the restricted ledger.\n\n\"I almost told Tomas he should stay until the next feast,\" he said.\n\n\"Would that have made him happier?\"\n\n\"I don't know. It would have made me feel better.\"\n\nThat was a difficult admission. I took his hand while we walked toward the river path.\n\n\"You're allowed to miss someone after they leave,\" I said. \"You don't get to make the missing into a duty they owe you.\"\n\nHe rubbed his thumb across my knuckles. \"Does that apply when you travel to see other people you love?\"\n\n\"Yes. I've told you I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. That doesn't mean I'll never be sad when we part. It means we can be honest without turning the sadness into a chain.\"\n\n\"I might need to be reminded.\"\n\n\"You can ask.\"\n\nWe passed Vel beside the market. She was still angry, and I was still angry about what she'd said to Tomas. I didn't approach her to demand an apology on his behalf. She had a right to understand her part in it at her own pace, even if I wished she'd start immediately.\n\nAt home, Maui put the returned carving on the shelf. I watched him choose not to throw it away.\n\n\"What if Tomas never comes back?\" he asked.\n\n\"Then the carving can still remind you that you cared.\"\n\nHe nodded. The little fish sat crookedly against the wall, plainly unfinished. Neither of us turned it around to make it look perfect.\n\nMaui watched her go.\n\n\"That hurt her,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I wanted her to understand.\"\n\n\"She might. Later. Or she might not.\"\n\nHe stared at the stones.\n\n\"I used to think having people admire me meant I had done something good. Now I know I can do something good and lose their admiration anyway.\"\n\nI touched his hand.\n\n\"That isn't a comfortable lesson.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nWe returned to Tideglass at dusk. Fen was setting up the tide chart for the next morning, and the first lanterns were flickering along the ridge. Maui offered to help carry the stand. Fen accepted without ceremony.\n\nFor a while that was enough.\n\nThe next day Vel arrived at our door with the little wooden fish Maui had once given her. She held it out.\n\n\"I don't want this anymore,\" she said.\n\nMaui took it.\n\n\"I'm sorry it upset you.\"\n\n\"It didn't upset me. It made me think you were someone who would always protect us.\"\n\n\"I will help when I can.\"\n\n\"That isn't the same.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said.\n\nShe left.\n\nHe turned the fish over in his hand. It was badly carved, with one eye much larger than the other. I remembered him making it on a rainy afternoon and declaring it a masterpiece.\n\n\"Want me to tell you she was wrong?\" I asked.\n\nHe shook his head.\n\n\"Not right now.\"\n\nWe sat on the steps.\n\n\"I liked being the person she thought I was,\" he admitted. \"Even though he didn't exist.\"\n\nI leaned against his shoulder.\n\n\"People can be disappointed when you don't belong to their story.\"\n\n\"Even you?\"\n\n\"Especially me. That's why I have to keep asking what you want instead of deciding what your legend means.\"\n\nHe put the fish beside the door.\n\n\"Do you still want me?\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Even without a crowd?\"\n\n\"Preferably without a crowd.\"\n\nHe laughed, the sound small and tired.\n\nI kissed him, gently, then pulled back when he seemed to need room to breathe.\n\nThat evening he put the returned fish on the shelf beside the green notebook from my old room. Two objects from people who had wanted different things. Neither one had to be thrown away to make the lesson disappear.\n\nIn the morning I checked the departure notice.\n\nThe festival banner had been moved to another wall.\n\nSomeone had written beneath the instructions, in ordinary dark ink:\n\nLeaving is not a debt.\n\nI didn't know who had written it.\n\nI was glad someone had.","word_count":1395},"maui-28":{"number":28,"title":"The Spare Key","content":"Neri offered me the key in a velvet pouch.\n\nThat was how I knew something was wrong.\n\nNeri was the kind of person who wrapped fragile archive seals in plain paper and put their own lunch in a tin that had lost its lid. Velvet meant ceremony, and ceremony usually meant somebody was trying to make a decision look less like a decision.\n\n\"What's that?\" I asked.\n\n\"A spare key to the lower records cabinet.\"\n\n\"Why do you want me to have it?\"\n\n\"Because you ask good questions.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"And because,\" Neri continued, \"the maintenance records may be important to understanding the second bell. If I am unavailable, someone should be able to open the cabinet.\"\n\nThe cabinet stood in Moonroot Archive, beneath a narrow window that let in just enough light to reveal the dust on its handles. Inside were copies of the old service-route plans, private correspondence whose release had not been authorized, and the burned ledger with the scratched-out signature.\n\nI looked at the pouch.\n\n\"Who else has a key?\"\n\nNeri blinked.\n\n\"Me.\"\n\n\"Who knows you're giving me one?\"\n\nThey hesitated.\n\nThat answered the question.\n\nI set the pouch down on the table.\n\n\"I can't take it like this.\"\n\nNeri looked hurt.\n\n\"I trust you.\"\n\n\"I know. I'm grateful. But trust isn't the same as authority.\"\n\nSeverus was cataloging a collection in the next room. He didn't turn around, though I could tell by the way his pen stopped that he'd heard every word.\n\nNeri folded their hands.\n\n\"Someone has to be able to open the cabinet in an emergency.\"\n\n\"Then we need an emergency procedure. Not a secret transfer of a key to somebody you like.\"\n\nThey looked toward the window.\n\n\"I thought it would be simpler.\"\n\n\"It would be simpler for us. Not for the people whose records are in there.\"\n\nI heard footsteps behind me.\n\nSeverus appeared in the doorway.\n\n\"An inconvenient distinction,\" he said.\n\nNeri sighed.\n\n\"You two are impossible.\"\n\n\"Correct,\" Severus replied.\n\nWe brought the question to the archive committee. The meeting took place in the same room where Severus's private letters had once been opened without permission. The memory made everyone careful, which was one good use of discomfort.\n\nThe committee chair, Vale, argued that the cabinet should have two keyholders.\n\n\"Two isn't an accountability system,\" I said. \"It's a number.\"\n\nTamsin, invited because the old service records contained structural plans, drew a diagram of the cabinet's contents and the reasons somebody might need access. Public maintenance documents could be copied and held openly. Private correspondence required separate handling. Emergency structural records needed a process that did not depend on Neri being awake.\n\nFen added a note about flood conditions.\n\n\"If water is rising,\" they said, \"we can't wait for an argument about who has the right to read a tide chart.\"\n\nWe separated the records into categories. The tide charts and structural inspection drawings would be accessible to the emergency team with a logged sign-out. Private letters would remain sealed unless their recipients authorized inspection or a narrowly documented immediate danger justified it.\n\nThe burned ledger would remain available to the evidence team under supervision. Nobody could scrape it further in pursuit of a satisfying name.\n\nThen we designed the key record.\n\nIt was a simple page: the date, the person opening the cabinet, the specific reason, which documents were accessed, whether copies were made, and when everything was returned.\n\nNo romantic histories. No traveler destinations. No list of who had looked worried when somebody arrived.\n\nMaui came to the meeting late, carrying a basket of bread from Mara.\n\nHe looked at the diagram.\n\n\"Can we put two locks on it?\" he asked.\n\nDanse, who had come for the structural records, looked up.\n\n\"That might increase security.\"\n\nEdda cleared her throat.\n\n\"Or make it harder for someone who can't reach the top latch.\"\n\nDanse winced.\n\nMaui put the bread down.\n\n\"Right. Not that.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nAfter three hours, the committee approved a revised process. Neri would continue as archive steward. Severus would supervise evidence handling in his assigned hours. Tamsin and Fen could request emergency structural access using the public log. A second key would be kept in a sealed case in an accessible location, with a tamper-evident record.\n\nNeri folded the velvet pouch and tucked it beneath the minutes. I could see the disappointment in their face. They had wanted to trust me with something important, and my refusal had made them wonder whether I trusted them back.\n\n\"Could we go through what would happen if there were a flood tonight?\" I asked.\n\nThey looked up. \"Of course.\"\n\nWe traced the process on the updated chart. Fen would report the water level to the public emergency lead. Tamsin could request a structural inspection through the documented route. The archive steward would authorize access to the maintenance plans, and the request would be logged even if the decision had to be made urgently.\n\n\"What if you're asleep?\" I asked Neri.\n\n\"Then the named deputy answers. That's what we forgot to write down.\"\n\nOrin added the deputy contact to the restricted procedure. Edda checked that reaching the log didn't require using an inaccessible high latch. Severus corrected a phrase that would have let anyone claiming urgency see private letters unrelated to the storm.\n\nMaui leaned against the doorway. \"So no single person can be trapped being the hero of the cabinet.\"\n\n\"Precisely,\" Neri said.\n\nHe looked pleased. I knew him well enough to recognize the wish to give a grand speech about not holding keys anymore.\n\nInstead he said, \"Would anybody like bread?\"\n\nWe all did.\n\nLater Neri found me by the fountain and apologized for offering me a responsibility I'd never asked for.\n\n\"I know you thought it was an honor,\" I said. \"But honor can become a weight when it isn't chosen.\"\n\n\"Then I will ask next time.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\nI went home with Maui and told him that being trusted didn't have to mean being indispensable. He looked at the tide chart beside our door.\n\n\"I'm still learning that one too.\"\n\nHe took my hand and waited before leaning closer. I welcomed the kiss, glad it was something we could choose without a committee or a spare key.\n\nI wasn't made keeper of anything.\n\nI felt relieved.\n\nWhen the meeting ended, Neri waited by the door.\n\n\"I thought you were the right person,\" they said.\n\n\"To hold the key?\"\n\n\"To carry the responsibility.\"\n\nI looked at the pouch, still on the table.\n\n\"Maybe I can help without owning the decision.\"\n\nNeri smiled.\n\n\"You already did.\"\n\nOutside, the rain had started again. The old stone under Lanternwell Square smelled cool and mineral, and water ran down the channels toward Tideglass.\n\nMaui walked beside me.\n\n\"Do you ever wish somebody would just give you a mysterious key and tell you you're chosen?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"I could make you one.\"\n\n\"Please don't.\"\n\n\"Golden? With a jewel?\"\n\n\"Absolutely not.\"\n\n\"Three jewels?\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe took my hand.\n\nWe passed the northern arch, where the departure notice had been updated. Then we stopped at the fountain to watch Neri's team install the new public sign-out board.\n\nThe process wasn't perfect. Somebody would forget a signature someday. An emergency could still force a difficult judgment. But at least everyone would know what the key opened and who was answerable for using it.\n\nMaui looked toward the water.\n\n\"There's still a key you wear,\" he said.\n\nI touched the brass shape at my neck, the one that fit the old maintenance hatches.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Do you think it means you should open them?\"\n\n\"No. I think it means I can help when the people affected have made a safe plan.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"That isn't much of a legend.\"\n\n\"It'll have to do.\"\n\nHe leaned close enough to kiss my forehead.\n\n\"Maybe the best legends are the ones where nobody gets trapped.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Write that down before you forget it.\"\n\nHe pulled a scrap of paper from his pocket and did, in enormous letters that took up half the page.\n\nLater, at home, we hung the scrap beside the tide chart.\n\nIt was a terrible piece of calligraphy.\n\nIt was a very good rule.","word_count":1374}}};for(const s of lib.series){const e=revisions[s.series_slug]||{};for(const c of s.chapters){const r=e[c.id];if(!r||c.chapter_number!==r.number)continue;c.content=r.content;c.word_count=r.word_count;c.title=r.title;}}})();
